FC Copenhagen Women have a =E2=80=98startup-like=E2=80=99 brand. Now they n=
eed results


A famous name and record attendances have given FCK a platform but a diffic=
ult start in Denmark=E2=80=99s top-flight shows success on the pitch is not=
 guaranteed


=E2=80=98Around 80-90% of the girls have never played in the top league so =
it=E2=80=99s a challenge.=E2=80=99 FC Copenhagen Women huddle before their =
match against Br=C3=B8ndby earlier this month. Photograph: Freja Borne


Sophie Downey


On an August day in 2024, 5,165 fans gathered at Vanl=C3=B8se Idr=C3=A6tspa=
rk to watch FC Copenhagen (FCK) take on B73 Slagelse in their first league =
match. For Denmark=E2=80=99s most successful men=E2=80=99s club, with 16 le=
ague titles, it marked the realisation of their desire to establish a women=
=E2=80=99s team, through collaboration with the existing FC Dams=C3=B8, and=
 an ambitious project with aims of having a wide-reaching impact on and off=
 the pitch.

The 26 months since have brought a whirlwind of success, the club securing =
back-to-back promotions to reach the A-Liga. Now they are in the top divisi=
on and =E2=80=9Cplaying with the big dogs=E2=80=9D, as their head of women=
=E2=80=99s football, Romaney Pinnock, coins it, and they must adapt to stay=
 there.


